245:### D-G â€” The live proof: `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/proof-local.ts` â†’ `proof-local.txt`.
246:Evidence-gathering, run once, never a guarding test. It REUSES the predecessor's hardened
247:redaction helpers (query-value scrub, JWT-shaped-substring scrub, fragment strip â€” the
248:[A6]/[L4] lessons) rather than rewriting them, and the executor inspects the transcript for
249:credential residue before committing it. Checks:
250:
251:  (a) WRONG PASSWORD: password-grant sign-in with a bad password â†’ 4xx whose `error_code` is
252:  the credential refusal (expected `invalid_credentials`; read from the IN-MEMORY body because
253:  the redactor blanks `error_code`; if the live code differs the check FAILS, the executor
254:  pins the measured code and re-runs â€” the fail-and-re-pin protocol). The body carries no
255:  `access_token`, no `refresh_token`, no `session` (the widened credential guard from the
256:  predecessor's [L3]). The REFUSED attempt adds no `auth.sessions` row on port 54322 â€”
257:  measured as an unchanged session-id set across the attempt, because one row already exists
258:  by then: following the emailed confirmation link is an implicit-flow sign-in and mints a
259:  session (MEASURED; re-pinned from this sentence's original "holds no row for the user" â€”
260:  `fix-rulings.md` ruling 1).
261:  (b) CORRECT PASSWORD (control): 200 with tokens; the `auth.sessions` row exists.
262:  (c) REVOCATION: `logout` with the live token â€” `?scope=local`, the shape the fixture's
263:  `signOut` mirrors, with a SIBLING session as the control that exactly one row disappeared;
264:  the vendor's DEFAULT scope is `global` (MEASURED: a plain logout emptied `auth.sessions`),
265:  recorded and asserted by nothing (`fix-rulings.md` ruling 3); then the SAME access token at
266:  `/auth/v1/user` â†’ MEASURED HTTP 403 (this sentence originally expected 401; re-pinned,
267:  `fix-rulings.md` ruling 2) carrying the pinned `session_not_found` error code â€” the
268:  load-bearing vendor claim that revocation ends access before the JWT's own expiry,
269:  confirmed; nothing shipped reads the status number (`callerFromAuthAnswer` accepts 2xx and
270:  refuses everything else); the revoked session's refresh token at the token
271:  endpoint â†’ refused; and the deployed `complete-signup` function called with the dead token â†’
272:  the auth-layer refusal (`resolveCaller` â†’ null), distinguished from a product refusal by
273:  also calling it once with a LIVE token (whose refusal, for an already-completed account, is
274:  the product's 4xx with a reason â€” both captured).
275:  (d) EXPIRY AND REFRESH-AFTER-EXPIRY: a TRANSIENT local config change lowers `jwt_expiry` to
276:  a few seconds, stack restarted and recorded; sign in; the token works; wait past expiry; the
277:  token at `/auth/v1/user` â†’ MEASURED HTTP 403 (originally expected 401; re-pinned,
278:  `fix-rulings.md` ruling 2) with the expired-token code captured verbatim; the deployed
279:  function refuses it â€” and that refusal is the PLATFORM's: `verify_jwt = true` rejects an
280:  expired JWT (body `Invalid JWT`) before `resolveCaller` runs, so check (c)'s REVOKED token,
281:  still signed and unexpired, is the one that reaches and binds `resolveCaller`
282:  (`fix-rulings.md` ruling 4); then the SAME session's refresh token â†’ 200 with a fresh access token that works
283:  at `/auth/v1/user` AND at the deployed function â€” refresh re-established access with no
284:  credentials, which is AT-001.13's live substance. THE SAME-SESSION-ROW PROBE (gate-1 ruling
285:  4): `auth.sessions` for this user is read with operator authority before and after the
286:  refresh â€” same row id, no new row â€” which is what binds the fixture's extend-the-same-row
287:  mirror; if the vendor rotates the row, the fail-and-re-pin protocol corrects the mirror to
288:  the measurement. Then `jwt_expiry` is RESTORED to 3600 and
289:  the stack restarted; the final `git diff` shows `supabase/config.toml` UNCHANGED â€” the
290:  transient change lives only in the transcript. Two failed restart attempts â†’ stop and
291:  report.
292:  (e) RESET, END TO END: a fresh address registers and confirms (the predecessor's catcher
293:  helpers); `/auth/v1/recover` â†’ the catcher holds the recovery email â†’ the link is extracted;
294:  a TAMPERED variant is followed FIRST and the old password still signs in (a token GoTrue
295:  never issued â€” no lifetime claim); the real link's flow SHAPE is MEASURED before it is
296:  asserted (token-in-fragment vs a PKCE code differ across CLI versions); the new password is
297:  set through the measured flow; then the old password â†’ the (a)-pinned credential refusal,
298:  and the new password â†’ 200. THE UNKNOWN-ADDRESS PROBE (gate-1 ruling 4): `/auth/v1/recover`
299:  is also called once for a never-registered address and the answer captured â€” expected the
300:  same 200 no-existence-oracle shape `requestPasswordReset` mirrors; if the live code differs,
301:  fail and re-pin.
302:  (f) if the mailer rate limit starves any email here, the predecessor's pre-authorized relief
303:  valve applies (raise the local limit, restart, record) â€” measured last item as NOT binding
304:  (the CLI did not push the key), so it is expected not to fire.
305:  (g) LINKED-VOLUNTEER CONTROL ACROSS THE REFACTORED EDGE (gate-1 ruling 2). The refactor
306:  moves the caller judgment into the pure module and leaves an untyped delegation in
307:  `edge.ts`; a delegation that pre-narrowed the Auth body (say `{ id }`) would lose
