14:---
15:
16:## Finding 1 â€” AT-001.13 declared green with no step testing automatic refresh
17:
18:> severity: high Â· plan.md:320
19:> "AT-001.13 is declared green even though no step implements or tests automatic refresh."
20:> "Explicitly calling `refreshSession` and manually exchanging a refresh token both pass with no
21:> timer, activity hook, or client auto-refresh. Because the plan marks .13 `backend`, the wiring
22:> leaf will not rerun it, so the entire planned gate can pass while continuous work still ends
23:> in forced re-login."
24:
25:**RULING: ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.**
26:
27:The defect is real, and it is the mark, not the missing timer. The criterion's "automatically"
28:is the client SDK's behaviour: supabase-js schedules the refresh; no code in this tree does.
29:The plan's per-id table says so honestly â€” but honesty is not ownership. The manifest's wiring
30:leaf finds its work by the `ui` mark (`loop/decomp/req-001.md` D2.LW: "wired re-run of D1/D2
31:ui-tagged P0s"), so a `backend` mark on .13 orphans the "automatically" clause: no dev leaf
32:ever exercises it, and the first thing that would is the requirement's far-away
33:integration-tier gate. That is the reviewer's point, and it stands.
34:
35:The remedy the finding implies â€” implement or test a timer at loop tier â€” is not the remedy we
36:use. No client exists in this tree. A fixture that scheduled its own refresh would grade the
37:fixture, which is the puppet anti-pattern this harness exists to delete.
38:
39:The fix: **AT-001.13's surface mark becomes `ui`.** The mark is the machine-readable handoff â€”
40:the wiring leaf must now find .13 and re-run it wired, where the real supabase-js client's
41:automatic refresh is the thing under test. D-I is amended to say this, the per-id table's
42:"not proved" cell for .13 now names the handoff instead of only the gap, and the body's own
43:comment states which half each tier proves. The loop-tier body still proves this leaf's half:
44:refresh extends a session with no credentials while an unrefreshed sibling of the same instant
45:dies.
46:
47:## Finding 2 â€” the live checks do not bind GitHub-handle extraction across the refactored edge
48:
49:> severity: high Â· plan.md:80
50:> "The live checks do not bind GitHub-handle extraction across the untyped `edge.ts` bridge
51:> changed by the refactor."
52:> "The selftest and fixture call `callerFromAuthAnswer` directly, while live checks (c)/(d)
53:> require only a usable caller id. An edge delegation that passes `{ id }` instead of the
54:> complete Auth body would pass every planned check but yield `githubHandle: null`, breaking
55:> deployed volunteer completion and the already-green AT-001.04/.05 paths. A live
56:> linked-volunteer control must cross the refactored edge."
57:
58:**RULING: ACCEPT.**
59:
60:Correct, and the sharpest finding of the gate. No type-checker covers `edge.ts` (its own
61:header says so). The refactor moves the judgment out and leaves an untyped delegation behind;
62:a delegation that pre-narrows the Auth body would lose `identities[]`, every planned check
63:would stay green, and the deployed volunteer gate would refuse every linked volunteer. The
64:predecessor item proved a linked volunteer through the DEPLOYED `complete-signup` (its check
65:(b)), but that evidence graded the pre-refactor bridge â€” this refactor supersedes it.
66:
67:The fix: a new live check **(g)** in D-G â€” a linked-volunteer control across the refactored
68:edge. The recipe exists and is proven: `fabricateGithubIdentity` in
69:`loop/items/AI4DEV-58/proof-local.ts` (line ~140) inserts the identity row into
70:`auth.identities` with operator authority. This item's variant follows the confirmations-on
71:order: register, confirm via the catcher, fabricate the identity, sign in, then call the
72:DEPLOYED `complete-signup` with the live token and assert completion succeeds and the profile
73:row carries the handle and the shipped stub's exact statistics. A pre-narrowed bridge fails
74:this check; nothing else in the plan would catch it.
75:
76:## Finding 3 â€” the .12/.13 bodies complete signup on the registration session
77:
78:> severity: medium Â· plan.md:157
79:> "The AT-001.12 and AT-001.13 bodies complete signup using the registration session before
80:> performing a post-confirmation sign-in."
81:> "Under the plan's own premise at lines 117-122, confirmations-on signup returns no live
82:> session; nevertheless both setups order registration â†’ confirmation â†’ completion â†’ sign-in.
83:> Since completion requires authentication, these bodies depend on the fixture-only
84:> registration handle to create the account used by subsequent write assertions, contradicting
85:> the claimed live public order. Sign-in must precede completion."
86:
87:**RULING: ACCEPT.**
88:
89:An internal contradiction, correctly caught. D-C promises every NEW body follows the live
90:public order; D-E's .12 and .13 setups then complete signup before signing in â€” on exactly the
91:registration-minted session the live stack never issues. The fix is the reviewer's own:
92:reorder both setups to register â†’ confirm â†’ sign in â†’ complete under the signed-in session â†’
93:then the expiry, revocation and refresh games. For .13, both sign-ins happen first and
94:completion runs under one of them; the account is then completed for both sessions, which is
95:what the paired writes need. AT-001.38 and AT-001.14 never complete signup and are unaffected.
96:(AT-001.09's deliberate opposite order is the PREDECESSOR's body, stated and reasoned there;
97:nothing here touches it.)
98:
99:## Finding 4 â€” "every new vendor mirror is live-bound" overreaches its cited checks
100:
101:> severity: medium Â· plan.md:282
102:> "Step 2's blanket claim that every new vendor mirror is live-bound cites checks that do not
103:> measure several promised behaviors."
104:> "Checks (a)/(b) measure email/password sign-in issuance, not registration, provider, or
105:> administrator issuance; registration is expressly known to diverge. Check (d) proves
106:> refreshed access but not the promised same-session-row/session-id behavior. Check (e)
107:> exercises recovery for an existing address, not `requestPasswordReset` always succeeding for
108:> an unknown one. The fixture header could therefore label these mirrors bound and satisfy the
109:> done-criterion while their stated vendor behavior remains unmeasured; each needs a probe, a
110:> narrower contract, or an explicit unbound label."
111:
112:**RULING: ACCEPT** â€” applying the reviewer's own menu, one choice per mirror:
113:
114:- **Issuance** â€” narrower contract plus explicit unbound labels. (a)/(b) bind SIGN-IN issuance
115:  and the `auth.sessions` row, and the label now says sign-in only. Registration issuance is
116:  the DECLARED divergence (the fixture mints at registration; the live stack does not) â€” it
117:  stays in the honest-gap paragraph, never labelled bound. Provider issuance: unbound, no
118:  OAuth credential exists â€” same standing as the header's existing mirror 4. Administrator
119:  issuance: recipe-mirrored and unbound â€” live admin creation issues no session; nothing
120:  asserts one; the label says so, following the header's administrator paragraph precedent.
121:- **Refresh, same-session-row** â€” a probe. Check (d) gains one operator read: `auth.sessions`
122:  for the user before and after the refresh, asserting the same session row persists (no new
123:  row, same id). If the vendor rotates the row instead, the fail-and-re-pin protocol applies
124:  and the fixture mirror is corrected to match the measurement.
125:- **Reset for an unknown address** â€” a probe. Check (e) gains one call: `/auth/v1/recover`
126:  for a never-registered address, expecting the same 200 shape â€” the no-existence-oracle
127:  behaviour `requestPasswordReset` mirrors. Captured, one HTTP call.
128:
129:Step 2's done-criterion is rewritten accordingly: every mirror is named with what binds it OR
130:an explicit unbound label â€” the standard the fixture header already practises.
