72:## 2. Decisions
73:
74:### D-A â€” No migration, no product session store. Sessions are Supabase Auth's own machinery.
75:Expiry, revocation, refresh and password reset are all GoTrue facts: `auth.sessions` rows, JWT
76:expiry, refresh tokens, recovery links. This leaf ships **no migration and no new table**. The
77:consequence is the same as the verification leaf's D-A: there is no schema or RLS surface to
78:prove here, and the live proof reads `auth.sessions` as operator evidence only.
79:
80:### D-B â€” ONE shipped change: the caller judgment becomes a pure module on the tested path.
81:AT-001.12's enforcement point at every deployed function is `resolveCaller`: Auth refuses the
82:dead token, and the function fails closed. Today that judgment lives in `edge.ts`, which no
83:type-checker covers and no test can import (Deno-only, stated in its header). This leaf
84:extracts the judgment â€” not the I/O â€” into a new pure module:
85:
86:- `supabase/functions/_shared/caller.ts`, under the same constraints as `accounts.ts`
87:  (relative imports only, no `Deno`, no I/O). It exports the `Caller` type and
88:  `callerFromAuthAnswer(status: number, user: unknown): Caller | null` â€” a caller only for a
89:  2xx status whose body carries a string `id`; the GitHub handle still derived through the
90:  shipped `extractGithubHandle`; **null for everything else** â€” non-2xx, missing id,
91:  non-string id, malformed body. Fail closed.
92:- `edge.ts`'s `resolveCaller` keeps the fetch and delegates the judgment to the module,
93:  re-exporting `type Caller` so neither edge function changes an import.
94:- A direct shape selftest, `tests/at/harness/shipped-caller.selftest.ts`, proves the
95:  fail-closed promise for every malformed shape (the same lane and the same reasoning as
96:  `shipped-verification.selftest.ts` â€” the vitest include `harness/**/*.selftest.ts` picks it
97:  up with zero script or CI change).
98:
99:This is the R3 pattern a third time: the fixture renders the vendor's answer shape and the
100:SHIPPED judgment decides, so the loop-tier green grades byte-for-byte the code every deployed
101:function runs on every authenticated request. Behaviour of the deployed functions is intended
102:to be UNCHANGED by the refactor; step 5 (c) and (d) drive a deployed function with dead and
103:live tokens, which is the live binding. TWO EDGES CHANGE (amended per gate-2 ruling 1; the
104:draft named one): a 2xx answer whose body is unparseable used to throw and surface as 502,
105:and now yields no caller and refuses; and a 2xx answer whose body is the JSON literal `null`
106:used to throw on the unguarded `.id` read (evidence quoted in `gate2-rulings.md`) and now
107:yields no caller and refuses. Every other parseable body behaves exactly as before â€” a
108:property read on a boxed primitive yields `undefined`, which failed the string check then and
109:fails it now. Both edges are the fail-closed direction, not reachable through GoTrue itself,
110:named in the code. A blank-string
111:`id` remains accepted, exactly as before the refactor â€” preserved, not tightened
112:(`draft-rulings.md` ruling 2).
113:
114:### D-C â€” The fixture models Auth's session bookkeeping, as a named vendor mirror.
115:`_fixture.ts` gains a session store: `sessions: Map<sessionId, { userId, expiresAt(ms),
116:revoked }>`. The adapter starts destructuring `clock` (already delivered by the harness).
117:
118:- **Issuance.** `register*`, `signInWithEmailPassword`, `signInWithProvider` and
119:  `provisionPlatformAdmin` mint a session row at `clock.now()` with
120:  `expiresAt = now + 3600s` â€” the mirror of `jwt_expiry = 3600`. The `Session` handle gains
121:  `sessionId: string` (no literal is constructed outside the fixture; the executor re-verifies
122:  before relying on it). **What binds issuance is NARROW (gate-1 ruling 4):** checks (a)/(b)
123:  bind SIGN-IN issuance and its `auth.sessions` row, and nothing wider. Registration issuance
124:  is the declared divergence â€” the live stack mints none until the address confirms â€” and is
125:  never labelled bound. Provider issuance and administrator issuance are labelled UNBOUND in
126:  the fixture header: no OAuth credential exists here, and live administrator creation issues
127:  no session; nothing in this suite asserts either.
128:- **Revocation.** New member `signOut(session)` marks the row revoked â€” the mirror of
129:  `POST /auth/v1/logout` deleting the session.
130:- **Refresh.** New member `refreshSession(session)` â€” valid only while the session is not
131:  revoked, INCLUDING after access expiry (the mirror of a refresh token outliving the access
132:  token, which is the entire point of refresh); it extends the SAME session row's `expiresAt`
133:  to `now + 3600s` and returns the same-`sessionId` handle. It takes NO credentials â€” that is
134:  structural, not asserted. Refresh-token ROTATION and the reuse interval are NOT modelled: no
135:  criterion reads them, and modelling vendor semantics nothing asserts is how retired ground
136:  creeps back in. The same-session-row detail is BOUND by a probe in check (d) â€” an operator
137:  read of `auth.sessions` before and after the live refresh, asserting the same row persists
138:  (gate-1 ruling 4); if the vendor rotates the row instead, the fail-and-re-pin protocol
139:  corrects this mirror to the measurement.
140:- **Validation, uniform.** Every session-taking operation (`completeSignup`,
141:  `createOrganization`, `sendDiscoveryMessage`, `linkGithubIdentity`) resolves its caller the
142:  way the deployed functions do: a known, unrevoked, unexpired session renders the canonical
143:  `/auth/v1/user` shape with status 200; anything else renders status 401 with no user; the
144:  SHIPPED `callerFromAuthAnswer` judges the answer, and a null caller refuses **without
145:  writing**. WHICH sessions are live is the vendor mirror; THAT a dead answer yields no caller
146:  is the shipped judgment on the tested path. The refusal reason for a dead session says
147:  sign-in again is required â€” fixture bookkeeping wording, not asserted verbatim by any body.
148:- **Session read-back.** New member `sessionsOf(accountId): Promise<{ sessionId: string }[]>`
149:  returns the account's unrevoked, unexpired sessions â€” the mirror of reading `auth.sessions`
150:  with operator authority. AT-001.38's "no authenticated session is created" needs it: a
151:  refusal's own return value cannot show that nothing was minted.
152:- **No behaviour change for the nine green ids.** No existing body advances the clock, and
153:  every existing body uses its session within one frozen instant, so uniform validation
154:  changes nothing green. Step 0's baseline and step 3's full run are the check.
155:
156:**The issuance-at-registration gap is inherited, restated, and narrowed by the bodies.** The
157:live stack with confirmations on issues no session at signup; the fixture keeps minting one at
158:registration because every existing body depends on it. The contract comment that names
159:session issuance "D2.L2's subject" is rewritten to state the remaining divergence plainly.
160:Each NEW body narrows it by following the live public order: register, use the emailed
161:verification link, and only then play the session and password games.
162:
163:### D-D â€” Password reset mirrors, with the retired-.15 line drawn exactly.
164:Three new members, all vendor mirrors:
165:
166:- `requestPasswordReset(email)` â€” always `{ ok: true }`, the mirror of `/auth/v1/recover`
167:  answering 200 regardless of existence (AT-001.21's no-existence-oracle shape, kept here for
168:  the same reason the sign-in refusal is single-reasoned). For a registered email/password
169:  user it mints a reset link.
170:- `emailedPasswordResetLink(email)` â€” the link, or null when none was emailed.
171:- `completePasswordReset(link, newPassword)` â€” the emailed link updates the password;
172:  a link that was never issued returns `{ ok: false }` and changes nothing. **No expiry, no
173:  single use, no resend is modelled or asserted** â€” the link is not cleared on use, exactly as
174:  verification links are not. The never-issued negative is not retired-.15 ground: it guards
175:  this test's own oracle (a link-shaped string that reset an account it never belonged to
176:  would make "completing the emailed flow is what changes the password" mean nothing).
177:
178:Whether a live password change revokes other sessions is NOT modelled and NOT asserted â€” no
179:criterion reads it, and `secure_password_change` sits false in the local config.
