103:## Ruling 4 â€” terra [4]: a resend retains the first reset link
104:
105:> terra, medium, `_fixture.ts:833`: "A second password-reset request retains the first link in
106:> `byPasswordResetLink`, so the earlier link remains usable after a resend. â€¦ Reset A â†’ reset
107:> B â†’ complete reset A succeeds, which defines a resend policy even though reset-link resend
108:> semantics are retired and explicitly declared unmodeled."
109:
110:**ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** The mechanism is real: a re-request mints a new link,
111:overwrites `user.passwordResetLink`, and leaves the earlier map entry alive. But the remedy
112:the finding points toward â€” invalidating the earlier link â€” would MODEL resend-invalidation,
113:which is retired AT-001.15 ground (acceptance line 30) that this item is forbidden to model
114:or assert. No body requests a reset twice, and none may assert either retention or
115:invalidation without re-entering the retired ground, so the behaviour is unobservable inside
116:the rules. The defect that remains is the record's: the retention is an unstated behaviour on
117:retired ground. Remedy: one comment addition at `requestPasswordReset` naming it â€” a
118:re-request leaves any earlier link in the map, deliberately unmodeled and unasserted, because
119:clearing it would be the retired resend semantics. No behaviour change.
