173:  // body first with `response.json()` would make an Auth outage that answers HTML â€” a proxy's error
174:  // page â€” throw here and become a 502 where it used to be a 401. An unparseable body is handed
175:  // over as `null` instead, so the status still decides and the deployed refusal path is
176:  // byte-for-byte what it was.
177:  const text = await response.text();
178:  let user: unknown = null;
179:  try {
180:    user = JSON.parse(text) as unknown;
181:  } catch {
182:    // Left as `null`, which `callerFromAuthAnswer` reads as no caller. Nothing is judged here.
183:    //
184:    // TWO EDGES DO CHANGE, and both are named rather than glossed. The first is this one: a 2xx
185:    // whose body is UNPARSEABLE used to throw and surface as a 502; it now refuses as a 401. The
186:    // second belongs to the parse below and is named here so the pair reads together: a 2xx whose
187:    // body is the JSON literal `null` parsed cleanly, and the old unguarded `.id` read on `null`
188:    // threw a TypeError which `edgeHandler` turned into a 502; it now yields no caller and refuses
189:    // as a 401. (The old code is quoted from the committed history in
190:    // `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/gate2-rulings.md`, which is where that reading was done.)
191:    //
192:    // AND THE CLAIM IS EXHAUSTIVE, which is the part that took a second reader to establish. Every
193:    // OTHER parseable body behaves exactly as it did: a property read on a boxed primitive â€” a
194:    // number, a string, a boolean â€” yields `undefined`, and an array has no `id` either, so all of
195:    // them failed the string check then and fail it now. `null` is the one parseable body whose old
196:    // behaviour differed.
