49: * WHAT A BROWSER IS ALLOWED TO SEND, and why this list is exactly this list.
50: *
51: * A signup from the app's own origin is a cross-origin authenticated JSON request, so the browser
52: * sends a preflight first and refuses to make the real call unless the answer permits the method
53: * and every header. `supabase-js`'s `functions.invoke` sends `authorization`, `apikey`,
54: * `content-type` and `x-client-info`; omitting any one of them fails the preflight, and the failure
55: * surfaces in the browser as a network error with no reason attached.
56: *
57: * THE ORIGIN IS `*` DELIBERATELY, and it is not a shortcut. An allow-list would need the deployed
58: * app origins, which are not knowable from this tree — inventing one would be a guess wearing the
59: * costume of a security control. It is safe here for a reason specific to these two endpoints:
60: * BOTH AUTHENTICATE BY `Authorization` HEADER AND NEITHER READS A COOKIE, so a hostile page that
61: * reaches them carries no ambient authority; it would have to already hold the user's access token,
62: * and if it holds that it does not need a browser. This is the Supabase standard posture for edge
63: * functions. A deployment that later authenticates by cookie must revisit this line first.
64: */
65:const CORS_HEADERS: Record<string, string> = {
66:  'access-control-allow-origin': '*',
67:  'access-control-allow-headers': 'authorization, apikey, content-type, x-client-info',
68:  'access-control-allow-methods': 'POST, OPTIONS',
69:};
70:
71:export function json(body: unknown, status: number): Response {
72:  return new Response(JSON.stringify(body), {
73:    status,
74:    headers: { ...CORS_HEADERS, 'content-type': 'application/json' },
75:  });
76:}
77:
78:/** A refusal a caller can act on: the reason travels, never a bare status. */
79:export function refusal(reason: string, status: number): Response {
80:  return json({ ok: false, reason }, status);
81:}
82:
83:/**
84: * The two conventions EVERY entry point here needs, applied once instead of twice.
85: *
86: *   * THE PREFLIGHT. Both functions refuse any method that is not POST, so without this an
87: *     `OPTIONS` would be answered 405 and no browser would ever reach the real call.
88: *   * NOTHING ESCAPES AS A BARE STATUS. A rejected `fetch` (Auth or the Data API unreachable) or a
89: *     non-JSON success body throws, and an exception out of a `Deno.serve` handler becomes a 500
90: *     with no body — precisely the case where a caller most needs to tell a refusal from an outage.
91: *     Anything thrown becomes a shaped 502 carrying a sentence, which is this file's standing
92: *     contract: the reason travels, never a bare status.
93: */
94:export function edgeHandler(
95:  name: string,
96:  handler: (request: Request) => Promise<Response>,
97:): (request: Request) => Promise<Response> {
98:  return async (request: Request): Promise<Response> => {
99:    if (request.method === 'OPTIONS') return new Response(null, { status: 204, headers: CORS_HEADERS });
100:    try {
101:      return await handler(request);
102:    } catch (error) {
103:      const detail = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
104:      return refusal(`${name} could not complete the request: ${detail}`, 502);
105:    }
106:  };
107:}
