, 1 red, 0 missing\n  EXPECTED: the run matches C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\expected\\req-016.json exactly (11 declared green, 1 declared red)\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n=========================================================================================\nSUMMARY - STEP 6\n=========================================================================================\nAll six commands exit 0.\n\n  bun run typecheck                                exit 0 - both configs clean\n  bun run at:selftest                              exit 0 - 10 files, 257 tests, all passed\n  bun run at:check req-001                         exit 0 - 37 P0 ids in bijection\n  bun run at:check req-016                         exit 0 - 12 P0 ids in bijection\n  bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect   exit 0 - 37 P0: 9 green, 28 red, 0 missing\n  bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop --expect   exit 0 - 12 P0: 11 green, 1 red, 0 missing\n\nAGAINST THE BASELINE (loop/items/AI4DEV-59/baseline.txt, the unchanged tree):\n  req-001 went from 7 green / 30 red to 9 GREEN / 28 RED. The two new greens are AT-001.09\n          and AT-001.10, this leaf's own pair. That is exactly what plan step 3 requires.\n  req-016 is UNCHANGED: 11 green / 1 red, the same as the baseline's line 107.\n\nTHE KNOWN FLAKE DID NOT RECUR. The baseline appendix records a stale-lock race in\ntests/at/harness/runner.selftest.ts line 222 that failed 1 run in 6 on the UNCHANGED tree.\nat:selftest passed here on the first attempt, so no re-run was needed and no note about a\nrecurrence is owed.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":169,"totalLines":169,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\verify-final.txt","time":{"start":1786290414801,"end":1786290414812}},"id":"prt_fe734a3c4001wsjqUx2mr3s7tk","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe73498fb001ZU9sGUexvIAU6U"}}
loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10250:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:27:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290427717,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_DenOUDcbQnokzsf1dnYt1579","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\artifacts\\gate2-flash-output.md"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\artifacts\\gate2-flash-output.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: All reads are complete. Here is my review.\n2: \n3: ---\n4: \n5: ## CODE REVIEW — AI4DEV-59 D2.L1 (email verification + unverified-write gate), branch `nirdrang/ai4dev-59-...-d2l1` at the pinned commit\n6: \n7: I read the whole diff surface: `verification.ts`, the selftest, `_contract.ts`, `_fixture.ts`, both new bodies, `_pending.ts`, `expected/req-001.json`, `pending-ledger.txt`, `config.toml`, `proof-local.ts`, plus the amended plan, the acceptance file, the manifest, and the supporting harness files (`registry.ts`, `runner.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`, `package.json`, `accounts.ts`, `github.ts`, the AI4DEV-58 transcript).\n8: \n9: What I verified as sound, so the empty parts of this gate are visible as empty:\n10: \n11: - **The fail-closed oracle covers everything the module's header promises.** The selftest drives missing field, `null`, `undefined`, `''`, `'   '`, two numbers, `true`, object values, an array value, and non-object users (`null`, `undefined`, string, number, boolean, array) — all `false`; only non-empty strings `true`. `discoveryMessageAllowed` refuses a missing caller, `null`, `{}`, `'true'`, `1`, `'yes'`, `{}`, the near-miss key `email_verified`, and a string caller, and the single refusal reason matches `/verif/i` and `/email/i` — the same two patterns AT-001.10's body asserts.\n12: - **The fixture discipline holds.** Both product judgements are the shipped module's (`emailVerified` and `sendDiscoveryMessage` both derive through `emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(...))`, the gate is `discoveryMessageAllowed`, the refusal text is carried through unchanged). All five new SUT members are implemented. All four vendor mirrors are named in the mirror section; the provider-confirmed start is declared UNBOUND; the never-issued negative is explicitly distinguished from retired AT-001.11, and the used link is deliberately not cleared (no single-use semantics). No test reads a provider user's verified state; both bodies register by email/password.\n13: - **AT-001.10's discriminating pair is real.** A refuse-everybody gate dies on the second half (the SAME send must succeed after the link); a write-before-refusing gate dies on the `discoveryMessagesBy` read-back (the fixture's write happens only on allow). The unverified control assertion attributes the refusal correctly.\n14: - **AT-001.09 implements plan D-F in order** — fresh-unverified, typed (row asserted for both `ngo` and `volunteer`, the volunteer branch genuinely passing the GitHub gate), still-unverified after completion, never-issued negative, emailed link flips, type survives. The live proof drives the opposite order (verify-then-complete) per the plan.\n15: - **Bookkeeping is true.** `expected/req-001.json` is 9 green / 28 red; `_pending.ts`'s counts (28/30/33, nine written) are right and `D2_L1` is removed with an explanatory note; the pending ledger's 28 lines match the manifest leaf-by-leaf (I checked each `verify:` line in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`), the coverage line, and the 37 total; the config comment's ownership claims match the manifest's D2.L1 and the plan's recorded baseline. No stale \"pending\" or \"reserved\" statements remain in the suite.\n16: - **The refactor is behavior-neutral for the seven green bodies**: `extractGithubHandle` reads only `identities`, which `renderAuthUser` renders identically to the previous inline shape; the added `email_confirmed_at` field is invisible to it.\n17: - The selftest-lane placement claims check out (`vitest.config.ts` line 16, `at:selftest`'s `harness/` filter, `at:verify`'s positional `suites/req-001/` filter, CI runs `at:selftest`).\n18: \n19: ### Findings\n20: \n21: **[1] severity: low (claimed-bound-but-unmeasured vendor mirror — the exact class the plan's step-2 criterion names)**    `tests/at/suites/req-001/_fixture.ts`:60-64\n22: - claim: Mirror 2 — \"a link that was never issued confirms nothing\" — is labeled **BOUND** by the live proof, but none of proof checks (a)–(d) ever attempts a never-issued link, so nothing in the named evidence measures the negative.\n23: - why it matters: The mirror section's own standard is \"a prediction has to say what would prove it wrong,\" and this binding is an inference about GoTrue's token design presented as a measurement. The same plan (section 7, ruling [3]; step 2 done-criterion) asserts \"a never-issued link returning `ok: false` … bound by step 5 (a)–(d)\", so the overstatement is in the amended plan text and the fixture header together. The positive half (link flips the column) is measured by (d); the negative half is not — and unlike the provider-confirmed start (unobtainable, hence UNBOUND), a bogus-link check is obtainable and simply wasn't added. What would settle it: add a step-5 check following a tampered/never-issued token and recording the refusal, or re-label mirror 2 as inference-bound in both the plan and the header.\n24: - unverified-runtime-claim: no (the absence of any bogus-link attempt in `proof-local.ts` is statically checkable; whether GoTrue would refuse is the runtime part).\n25: \n26: **[2] severity: low (redaction is shape-dependent where the header claims absolutes)**    `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`:122-126, 483-486, 102-120\n27: - claim: The header promises \"NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT\", but the followed redirect's `location` is printed after only fragment-stripping (`withoutFragment`), and `redact()` returns non-object values — including non-JSON response bodies — verbatim.\n28: - why it matters: If the stack's GoTrue redirects with a token-bearing query parameter rather than a fragment (token-hash/deep-link shapes do exactly this), that string lands in a transcript the plan pushes to GitHub — in a project whose history already includes a push-protection refusal over a transcript capturing a status command. The fragment-only assumption is a runtime claim that has never been measured (the script never ran). What would settle it: run the script once and inspect the `location` line and any non-JSON body in the transcript, or extend the redaction to strip query parameters whose names match the same `SENSITIVE` pattern.\n29: - unverified-runtime-claim: yes (depends on the never-measured redirect shape of the local GoTrue).\n30: \n31: **[3] severity: low (false statement in a rewritten header)**    `tests/at/suites/req-001/b-verification-and-sessions.test.ts`:6\n32: - claim: The rewritten header says \"The other four are section C's\", but AT-001.38 (wrong-password rejection) sits in **section B** of the acceptance file (`at-req-001.md` line 23, under \"## B. Email verification\"); only .12/.13/.14 are section C's.\n33: - why it matters: This item's own done-criterion is \"no comment in any touched file still states … a stale count\", and the header was rewritten in this diff — a reader following the sentence to the acceptance file finds it false for one of the four ids. The charitable reading (\".38 belongs to the sessions leaf\") is what the next clause says correctly (\"belong to the session-and-reset leaf (D2.L2)\"), so the fix is a one-word correction (\"the other four are the session-and-reset leaf's, three of them section C's\" or similar). Not a test-meaning defect.\n34: - unverified-runtime-claim: no.\n35: \n36: ### One observation, not a finding\n37: \n38: `proof-local.ts` is in no typecheck program — the root `tsconfig.json` includes only `src/**`/`vite.config.ts`/`eslint.config.js`, and `tests/at/tsconfig.json` covers only `tests/at`. That matches the predecessor's deliberate item-record placement, and any type-level mistake in it (e.g., a wrong mailpit/inbucket field name) fails loudly when the script runs rather than producing a false pass, so I don't count it as a defect — but it means the script's only validator is its first run, which is exactly why the \"WRITTEN AND NEVER RUN\" status of every check in it matters.\n39: \n40: Out-of-scope items (per the item's list) surfaced nothing that needs mentioning.\n41: \n42: CODE REVIEW: 3 FINDINGS\n\n(End of file - total 42 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"All reads are complete. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## CODE REVIEW — AI4DEV-59 D2.L1 (email verification + unverified-write gate), branch `nirdrang/ai4dev-59-...-d2l1` at the pinned commit\n\nI read the whole diff surface: `verification.ts`, the selftest, `_contract.ts`, `_fixture.ts`, both new bodies, `_pending.ts`, `expected/req-001.json`, `pending-ledger.txt`, `config.toml`, `proof-local.ts`, plus the amended plan, the acceptance file, the manifest, and the supporting harness files (`registry.ts`, `runner.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`, `package.json`, `accounts.ts`, `github.ts`, the AI4DEV-58 transcript).\n\nWhat I verified as sound, so the empty parts of this gate are visible as empty:\n\n- **The fail-closed oracle covers everything the module's header promises.** The selftest drives missing field, `null`, `undefined`, `''`, `'   '`, two numbers, `true`, object values, an array value, and non-object users (`null`, `undefined`, string, number, boolean, array) — all `false`; only non-empty strings `true`. `discoveryMessageAllowed` refuses a missing caller, `null`, `{}`, `'true'`, `1`, `'yes'`, `{}`, the near-miss key `email_verified`, and a string caller, and the single refusal reason matches `/verif/i` and `/email/i` — the same two patterns AT-001.10's body asserts.\n- **The fixture discipline holds.** Both product judgements are the shipped module's (`emailVerified` and `sendDiscoveryMessage` both derive through `emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(...))`, the gate is `discoveryMessageAllowed`, the refusal text is carried through unchanged). All five new SUT members are implemented. All four vendor mirrors are named in the mirror section; the provider-confirmed start is declared UNBOUND; the never-issued negative is explicitly distinguished from retired AT-001.11, and the used link is deliberately not cleared (no single-use semantics). No test reads a provider user's verified state; both bodies register by email/password.\n- **AT-001.10's discriminating pair is real.** A refuse-everybody gate dies on the second half (the SAME send must succeed after the link); a write-before-refusing gate dies on the `discoveryMessagesBy` read-back (the fixture's write happens only on allow). The unverified control assertion attributes the refusal correctly.\n- **AT-001.09 implements plan D-F in order** — fresh-unverified, typed (row asserted for both `ngo` and `volunteer`, the volunteer branch genuinely passing the GitHub gate), still-unverified after completion, never-issued negative, emailed link flips, type survives. The live proof drives the opposite order (verify-then-complete) per the plan.\n- **Bookkeeping is true.** `expected/req-001.json` is 9 green / 28 red; `_pending.ts`'s counts (28/30/33, nine written) are right and `D2_L1` is removed with an explanatory note; the pending ledger's 28 lines match the manifest leaf-by-leaf (I checked each `verify:` line in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`), the coverage line, and the 37 total; the config comment's ownership claims match the manifest's D2.L1 and the plan's recorded baseline. No stale \"pending\" or \"reserved\" statements remain in the suite.\n- **The refactor is behavior-neutral for the seven green bodies**: `extractGithubHandle` reads only `identities`, which `renderAuthUser` renders identically to the previous inline shape; the added `email_confirmed_at` field is invisible to it.\n- The selftest-lane placement claims check out (`vitest.config.ts` line 16, `at:selftest`'s `harness/` filter, `at:verify`'s positional `suites/req-001/` filter, CI runs `at:selftest`).\n\n### Findings\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\artifacts\\gate2-flash-output.md","text":"All reads are complete. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## CODE REVIEW — AI4DEV-59 D2.L1 (email verification + unverified-write gate), branch `nirdrang/ai4dev-59-...-d2l1` at the pinned commit\n\nI read the whole diff surface: `verification.ts`, the selftest, `_contract.ts`, `_fixture.ts`, both new bodies, `_pending.ts`, `expected/req-001.json`, `pending-ledger.txt`, `config.toml`, `proof-local.ts`, plus the amended plan, the acceptance file, the manifest, and the supporting harness files (`registry.ts`, `runner.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`, `package.json`, `accounts.ts`, `github.ts`, the AI4DEV-58 transcript).\n\nWhat I verified as sound, so the empty parts of this gate are visible as empty:\n\n- **The fail-closed oracle covers everything the module's header promises.** The selftest drives missing field, `null`, `undefined`, `''`, `'   '`, two numbers, `true`, object values, an array value, and non-object users (`null`, `undefined`, string, number, boolean, array) — all `false`; only non-empty strings `true`. `discoveryMessageAllowed` refuses a missing caller, `null`, `{}`, `'true'`, `1`, `'yes'`, `{}`, the near-miss key `email_verified`, and a string caller, and the single refusal reason matches `/verif/i` and `/email/i` — the same two patterns AT-001.10's body asserts.\n- **The fixture discipline holds.** Both product judgements are the shipped module's (`emailVerified` and `sendDiscoveryMessage` both derive through `emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(...))`, the gate is `discoveryMessageAllowed`, the refusal text is carried through unchanged). All five new SUT members are implemented. All four vendor mirrors are named in the mirror section; the provider-confirmed start is declared UNBOUND; the never-issued negative is explicitly distinguished from retired AT-001.11, and the used link is deliberately not cleared (no single-use semantics). No test reads a provider user's verified state; both bodies register by email/password.\n- **AT-001.10's discriminating pair is real.** A refuse-everybody gate dies on the second half (the SAME send must succeed after the link); a write-before-refusing gate dies on the `discoveryMessagesBy` read-back (the fixture's write happens only on allow). The unverified control assertion attributes the refusal correctly.\n- **AT-001.09 implements plan D-F in order** — fresh-unverified, typed (row asserted for both `ngo` and `volunteer`, the volunteer branch genuinely passing the GitHub gate), still-unverified after completion, never-issued negative, emailed link flips, type survives. The live proof drives the opposite order (verify-then-complete) per the plan.\n- **Bookkeeping is true.** `expected/req-001.json` is 9 green / 28 red; `_pending.ts`'s counts (28/30/33, nine written) are right and `D2_L1` is removed with an explanatory note; the pending ledger's 28 lines match the manifest leaf-by-leaf (I checked each `verify:` line in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`), the coverage line, and the 37 total; the config comment's ownership claims match the manifest's D2.L1 and the plan's recorded baseline. No stale \"pending\" or \"reserved\" statements remain in the suite.\n- **The refactor is behavior-neutral for the seven green bodies**: `extractGithubHandle` reads only `identities`, which `renderAuthUser` renders identically to the previous inline shape; the added `email_confirmed_at` field is invisible to it.\n- The selftest-lane placement claims check out (`vitest.config.ts` line 16, `at:selftest`'s `harness/` filter, `at:verify`'s positional `suites/req-001/` filter, CI runs `at:selftest`).\n\n### Findings\n\n**[1] severity: low (claimed-bound-but-unmeasured vendor mirror — the exact class the plan's step-2 criterion names)**    `tests/at/suites/req-001/_fixture.ts`:60-64\n- claim: Mirror 2 — \"a link that was never issued confirms nothing\" — is labeled **BOUND** by the live proof, but none of proof checks (a)–(d) ever attempts a never-issued link, so nothing in the named evidence measures the negative.\n- why it matters: The mirror section's own standard is \"a prediction has to say what would prove it wrong,\" and this binding is an inference about GoTrue's token design presented as a measurement. The same plan (section 7, ruling [3]; step 2 done-criterion) asserts \"a never-issued link returning `ok: false` … bound by step 5 (a)–(d)\", so the overstatement is in the amended plan text and the fixture header together. The positive half (link flips the column) is measured by (d); the negative half is not — and unlike the provider-confirmed start (unobtainable, hence UNBOUND), a bogus-link check is obtainable and simply wasn't added. What would settle it: add a step-5 check following a tampered/never-issued token and recording the refusal, or re-label mirror 2 as inference-bound in both the plan and the header.\n- unverified-runtime-claim: no (the absence of any bogus-link attempt in `proof-local.ts` is statically checkable; whether GoTrue would refuse is the runtime part).\n\n**[2] severity: low (redaction is shape-dependent where the header claims absolutes)**    `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`:122-126, 483-486, 102-120\n- claim: The header promises \"NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT\", but the followed redirect's `location` is printed after only fragment-stripping (`withoutFragment`), and `redact()` returns non-object values — including non-JSON response bodies — verbatim.\n- why it matters: If the stack's GoTrue redirects with a token-bearing query parameter rather than a fragment (token-hash/deep-link shapes do exactly this), that string lands in a transcript the plan pushes to GitHub — in a project whose history already includes a push-protection refusal over a transcript capturing a status command. The fragment-only assumption is a runtime claim that has never been measured (the script never ran). What would settle it: run the script once and inspect the `location` line and any non-JSON body in the transcript, or extend the redaction to strip query parameters whose names match the same `SENSITIVE` pattern.\n- unverified-runtime-claim: yes (depends on the never-measured redirect shape of the local GoTrue).\n\n**[3] severity: low (false statement in a rewritten header)**    `tests/at/suites/req-001/b-verification-and-sessions.test.ts`:6\n- claim: The rewritten header says \"The other four are section C's\", but AT-001.38 (wrong-password rejection) sits in **section B** of the acceptance file (`at-req-001.md` line 23, under \"## B. Email verification\"); only .12/.13/.14 are section C's.\n- why it matters: This item's own done-criterion is \"no comment in any touched file still states … a stale count\", and the header was rewritten in this diff — a reader following the sentence to the acceptance file finds it false for one of the four ids. The charitable reading (\".38 belongs to the sessions leaf\") is what the next clause says correctly (\"belong to the session-and-reset leaf (D2.L2)\"), so the fix is a one-word correction (\"the other four are the session-and-reset leaf's, three of them section C's\" or similar). Not a test-meaning defect.\n- unverified-runtime-claim: no.\n\n### One observation, not a finding\n\n`proof-local.ts` is in no typecheck program — the root `tsconfig.json` includes only `src/**`/`vite.config.ts`/`eslint.config.js`, and `tests/at/tsconfig.json` covers only `tests/at`. That matches the predecessor's deliberate item-record placement, and any type-level mistake in it (e.g., a wrong mailpit/inbucket field name) fails loudly when the script runs rather than producing a false pass, so I don't count it as a defect — but it means the script's only validator is its first run, which is exactly why the \"WRITTEN AND NEVER RUN\" status of every check in it matters.\n\nOut-of-scope items (per the item's list) surfaced nothing that needs mentioning.\n\nCODE REVIEW: 3 FINDINGS","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":42,"totalLines":42,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\artifacts\\gate2-flash-output.md","time":{"start":1786290427708,"end":1786290427715}},"id":"prt_fe734d52300138odhghX2JJQjN","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe734bb8d001jNvAF7FlrDJCWF"}}
loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10253:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:36:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290447546,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_bUQ6pzKQ2CwjqxzlcmLU1663","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\b-verification-and-sessions.test.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\b-verification-and-sessions.test.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * AT-REQ-001 sections B and C — email verification, the wrong-password path, sessions and reset.\n3:  *\n4:  * TWO OF THE SIX IDS HERE ARE NOW WRITTEN — AT-001.09 and AT-001.10, the verification leaf's own\n5:  * pair. The other four all belong to the session-and-reset leaf (D2.L2): three of them are section\n6:  * C's, and the wrong-password one is section B's, beside this leaf's pair. They are still declared,\n7:  * not faked, and `LEAF`/`notLanded` stay imported for them.\n8:  *\n9:  * `supabase/config.toml` now carries `[auth.email] enable_confirmations = true`. Turning it on is\n10:  * this leaf's own change, made in the same item as these two bodies. (The sentence this header\n11:  * used to carry said the opposite and reserved the flip for a later leaf; that leaf is this one,\n12:  * so the sentence is replaced rather than left to read as a promise nobody kept.)\n13:  *\n14:  * WHAT THE FLIP DOES AND DOES NOT REACH, said before the first assertion because it is the one\n15:  * thing a reader could get wrong here. The flip is a LIVE-STACK change: with confirmations on, the\n16:  * real GoTrue issues no session at signup and refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed. NONE\n17:  * of that is reachable from this file — the fixture adapter's storage is a Map and its `Session`\n18:  * has always been an identity handle rather than an access token. The live behaviour is measured\n19:  * once, by hand, in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`, checks (a), (c) and (d), and by nothing\n20:  * else in this item.\n21:  *\n22:  * WHICH LEAVES ONE HONEST GAP, and it is stated rather than hidden: at THIS tier a\n23:  * completed-but-unverified account is constructible, and AT-001.10's body constructs one on\n24:  * purpose. On the live stack the public path cannot reach that state. The gate is still the right\n25:  * thing to ship and to test, because decision-8 makes verification the WRITE PATH's own floor —\n26:  * \"the floor for any Discovery message\" — and not a property borrowed from the session layer.\n27:  * Non-public paths and configuration history both produce exactly this state, and a floor that\n28:  * only holds while the session layer holds is not a floor.\n29:  *\n30:  * NO EXPIRY, SINGLE-USE OR RESEND SEMANTICS ARE ASSERTED ANYWHERE BELOW. AT-001.11 is retired —\n31:  * `.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md` line 22: \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend\n32:  * semantics are not stated in REQ-001\" — so asserting any of them would be reviving a criterion\n33:  * that was deliberately withdrawn.\n34:  *\n35:  * SURFACE MARKS. AT-001.09 is marked `surface: 'ui'` because the verification screen is one of the\n36:  * four auth screens the manifest's wiring leaf names (\"signup, sign-in, verification, reset\"), so\n37:  * that leaf must find this id to re-run it with `--wired`. AT-001.10 stays `backend`: the Discovery\n38:  * composer is REQ-002/004's surface, it is not one of those four screens, and the wiring leaf has\n39:  * nothing of it to re-run.\n40:  */\n41: \n42: import { expect } from 'vitest';\n43: import { atTest } from './_bind.ts';\n44: import { LEAF, notLanded } from './_pending.ts';\n45: // THE TWO EMAIL-CAPABLE PUBLIC TYPES, read from the shipped vocabulary rather than spelled as a\n46: // pair of literals. AT-001.09's own words are \"EITHER account type that can register by email (NGO\n47: // and volunteer)\", and `PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES` is that set as a value: the platform\n48: // administrator is provisioned rather than signed up, so `parseAccountType` refuses it and it is\n49: // outside this criterion by construction. Iterating the constant makes \"either account type\" a\n50: // fact of the run instead of a claim in a test name.\n51: import { PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n52: \n53: /** The version string of the ToS + Platform Promise text these tests accept on the user's behalf. */\n54: const TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';\n55: /** The address the acknowledgment records — the same reported-not-verified value section A uses. */\n56: const CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';\n57: /** The password every email/password registration in this file uses. */\n58: const PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';\n59: \n60: atTest(\n61:   'AT-001.09',\n62:   'a fresh email/password signup of either account type is unverified until the link is used',\n63:   { surface: 'ui' },\n64:   async ({ open }) => {\n65:     const { w, sut } = await open();\n66: \n67:     // THE ORDERING IS DELIBERATE AND IS STATED SO IT CAN BE ATTACKED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. This\n68:     // body completes signup BEFORE verifying, so that the TYPED account is observably unverified —\n69:     // the criterion says \"the account ... is email-unverified until the emailed verification link\n70:     // is used\", and \"account\" read as the typed account is what makes clause 2 below meaningful.\n71:     // On the live stack with confirmations on, the public order is the other one: verify, then\n72:     // sign in, then complete. The live proof drives THAT order against the real stack, check (e).\n73:     // The two orders together cover both readings of \"account\", and neither contradicts any\n74:     // criterion.\n75:     for (const accountType of PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES) {\n76:       const email = w.email(`verify-${accountType}`);\n77: \n78:       // (1) A FRESH SIGNUP IS UNVERIFIED. This is the half that fails against an implementation\n79:       // which confirms addresses at registration, and it has to be read BEFORE anything else\n80:       // happens to the account.\n81:       const session = await sut.registerWithEmailPassword(email, PASSWORD);\n82:       expect(\n83:         await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),\n84:         `a fresh ${accountType} email/password signup must be email-unverified`,\n85:       ).toBe(false);\n86: \n87:       // The volunteer branch needs a linked GitHub identity before completion — that is AT-001.04's\n88:       // requirement, not this one's, and it is satisfied here rather than tested.\n89:       if (accountType === 'volunteer') {\n90:         await sut.linkGithubIdentity(session, 'riverside-verifier');\n91:       }\n92: \n93:       // (2) THE ACCOUNT IS TYPED, and the row says which type. Without this the loop would prove\n94:       // the same thing twice about two indistinguishable accounts, and \"either account type\" would\n95:       // be a claim in the test's name rather than a fact of the run.\n96:       const completion = await sut.completeSignup(\n97:         session,\n98:         {\n99:           accountType,\n100:           organizationName: accountType === 'ngo' ? 'Riverside Shelter' : undefined,\n101:           acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n102:         },\n103:         CLIENT_IP,\n104:       );\n105:       expect(completion, `the ${accountType} completion was refused`).toMatchObject({ ok: true });\n106:       if (!completion.ok) return;\n107:       expect(await sut.account(completion.accountId)).toEqual({\n108:         id: session.accountId,\n109:         accountType,\n110:       });\n111: \n112:       // (3) COMPLETING SIGNUP DOES NOT VERIFY THE ADDRESS. An implementation that flipped the flag\n113:       // as a side effect of completion would satisfy the final assertion of this test while making\n114:       // the emailed link decorative.\n115:       expect(\n116:         await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),\n117:         `completing ${accountType} signup must not verify the email address`,\n118:       ).toBe(false);\n119: \n120:       // (4) A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED FLIPS NOTHING. This is not a claim about link lifetime —\n121:       // retired AT-001.11's ground, deliberately untouched. It guards THIS TEST'S OWN ORACLE: if a\n122:       // link-shaped string verified an account it never belonged to, then \"using the emailed link\n123:       // is what flips it\" would mean nothing, because any string would flip anything.\n124:       const neverIssued = await sut.useVerificationLink('never-issued-link');\n125:       expect(neverIssued.ok, 'a verification link that was never issued must not succeed').toBe(false);\n126:       expect(\n127:         await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),\n128:         'a verification link that was never issued flipped the account to verified',\n129:       ).toBe(false);\n130: \n131:       // (5) USING THE EMAILED LINK IS WHAT FLIPS IT — the criterion's second clause, and the\n132:       // discriminating change. Everything else about the account is identical across this line.\n133:       const link = await sut.emailedVerificationLink(email);\n134:       expect(link, `no verification link was emailed to the ${accountType} address`).not.toBeNull();\n135:       const used = await sut.useVerificationLink(link!);\n136:       expect(used.ok, 'the emailed verification link was refused').toBe(true);\n137:       expect(\n138:         await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),\n139:         'using the emailed verification link did not flip the account to verified',\n140:       ).toBe(true);\n141: \n142:       // (6) VERIFICATION RE-TYPES NOTHING. The account is the same account, of the same type, and\n143:       // the only thing that changed is the one thing the criterion is about.\n144:       expect(await sut.account(completion.accountId)).toEqual({\n145:         id: session.accountId,\n146:         accountType,\n147:       });\n148:     }\n149:   },\n150: );\n151: \n152: atTest(\n153:   'AT-001.10',\n154:   'an unverified NGO account is blocked from Discovery messages with verification named as the remedy',\n155:   { surface: 'backend' },\n156:   async ({ open }) => {\n157:     const { w, sut } = await open();\n158: \n159:     // WHAT THIS TEST GRADES, said plainly: the SHIPPED decision\n160:     // `discoveryMessageAllowed` in `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`. There is NO\n161:     // Discovery route in this repository — it is REQ-002/004's — so `sendDiscoveryMessage` is a\n162:     // stand-in surface that exists to put that decision on a tested path. No green here says\n163:     // Discovery messaging is gated anywhere; it says the gate answers correctly and that a refusal\n164:     // writes nothing.\n165:     //\n166:     // AND THE STATE THIS BODY BUILDS IS NOT REACHABLE BY THE LIVE PUBLIC PATH. With confirmations\n167:     // on, GoTrue issues no session at signup, so a completed-but-unverified account cannot be\n168:     // produced by a visitor. The gate exists anyway, and the reason is decision-8: verification is\n169:     // \"the floor for any Discovery message\" — the write path's own rule, not one borrowed from the\n170:     // session layer. Non-public paths and configuration history reach this state, and the live\n171:     // auth-layer block is measured separately in the item's proof script, check (c).\n172:     const email = w.email('unverified-ngo');\n173:     const session = await sut.registerWithEmailPassword(email, PASSWORD);\n174:     const completion = await sut.completeSignup(\n175:       session,\n176:       {\n177:         accountType: 'ngo',\n178:         organizationName: 'Riverside Shelter',\n179:         acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n180:       },\n181:       CLIENT_IP,\n182:     );\n183:     expect(completion, 'the NGO completion was refused').toMatchObject({ ok: true });\n184:     if (!completion.ok) return;\n185: \n186:     // The control that makes the refusal below attributable. If the account were already verified,\n187:     // a refusal would be evidence of a broken gate rather than of a working one.\n188:     expect(\n189:       await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),\n190:       'this test is about an UNVERIFIED account; if it is verified, nothing below is about AT-001.10',\n191:     ).toBe(false);\n192: \n193:     const MESSAGE = 'Hello — we would like to talk about your project.';\n194: \n195:     // (1) THE BLOCK, AND THE REMEDY IT NAMES. \"the attempt is blocked with verification named as\n196:     // the remedy\": a refusal saying only \"forbidden\" would satisfy a weaker reading of the\n197:     // criterion and would tell the sender nothing about what to do next.\n198:     const blocked = await sut.sendDiscoveryMessage(session, MESSAGE);\n199:     expect(blocked.ok, 'an unverified account was allowed to send a Discovery message').toBe(false);\n200:     if (blocked.ok) return;\n201:     expect(blocked.reason, 'the refusal does not name verification').toMatch(/verif/i);\n202:     expect(blocked.reason, 'the refusal does not name the email address as what needs verifying').toMatch(/email/i);\n203: \n204:     // (2) THE BLOCKED ATTEMPT WROTE NOTHING. A block whose write happened anyway is not a block,\n205:     // and the refusal's own return value cannot show this — only a read-back can.\n206:     expect(\n207:       await sut.discoveryMessagesBy(session.accountId),\n208:       'the blocked Discovery message was recorded anyway',\n209:     ).toEqual([]);\n210: \n211:     // (3) THE DISCRIMINATING CONTROL. Verification is proved to be THE REMEDY by being the exact\n212:     // change that unblocks the exact same send. Without this half, a gate that refused everybody\n213:     // unconditionally would pass every assertion above.\n214:     const link = await sut.emailedVerificationLink(email);\n215:     expect(link, 'no verification link was emailed to the NGO address').not.toBeNull();\n216:     expect((await sut.useVerificationLink(link!)).ok, 'the emailed verification link was refused').toBe(true);\n217:     expect(\n218:       await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),\n219:       'using the emailed verification link did not flip the account to verified',\n220:     ).toBe(true);\n221: \n222:     const allowed = await sut.sendDiscoveryMessage(session, MESSAGE);\n223:     expect(allowed.ok, 'the SAME Discovery message was still refused after verification').toBe(true);\n224:     expect(\n225:       await sut.disco
